{
  "name": "What to Do Before You Refresh a Foreclosed Property Kitchen",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Refreshing a kitchen in a foreclosed property can be exciting, but hold your horses on the pretty stuff until you've done some groundwork! The very first thing to do is a thorough inspection for any underlying issues. We're talking leaks, electrical faults, signs of moisture, or structural problems. These aren't just minor fixes; they can really dictate the entire renovation timeline and budget. Imagine putting in new cabinets only to find a plumbing leak behind them later! It’s also crucial to decide what can realistically be refreshed versus what needs full replacement. Are the cabinets salvageable with just a good cleaning and some paint, or are they falling apart? Are the countertops still solid, or do they have water damage? You'll want to prioritize addressing any safety-related problems first, like damaged wiring or active leaks. In fact, for distressed properties, handling structural, electrical, plumbing, and moisture issues *before* any cosmetic updates like cabinet refacing or new paint is always the smart move. Getting this right upfront means your beautiful new kitchen will be built on a solid, safe foundation. HH Remodeling LLC can help you identify these critical issues from the start.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
